      {
        "id": 164127,
        "name": "Arcanist Lupus",
        "username": "Arcanist_Lupus",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arcanist_lupus/{size}/2169_2.png",
        "created_at": "2016-03-03T07:14:54.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"bobbertoriley\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/bobbertoriley/48/1910_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> bobbertoriley:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Indestructible, according to the rulebook, means it can't leave play (including to the hand). So, multiple tools is a valid state.</p>\n<p>And with Overdrive, you can get up to three of each into play. <img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/sunglasses.png?v=12\" title=\":sunglasses:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":sunglasses:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"></p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Which is, as far as I can tell, the least intuitive rule in the game, and I'm not entirely sure why it exists (besides, of course, to cause delightful shenanigans&nbsp;involving tools and FIxed Point) since as far as I know the only card that could remove a villain card from play without destroying it is Into the Stratosphere, which already cannot target indestructible cards.</p>",
        "post_number": 22,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:45:16.809Z",
