      {
        "id": 164132,
        "name": "Arcanist Lupus",
        "username": "Arcanist_Lupus",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arcanist_lupus/{size}/2169_2.png",
        "created_at": "2016-03-03T19:37:40.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Powerhound_2000\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/powerhound_2000/48/1859_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Powerhound_2000:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Spite returns cards to you hand that the other case that comes to mind. &nbsp;</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>True. &nbsp;I see that I'm not explaining my point properly.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>The reason that \"indestrutable\" exists as a keyword is because there are certain villain cards that would significantly break the game if they were to leave play, such as Spite's drugs, Safehouse, and Shrine of the Ennead. &nbsp;It is also used to make some villain cards more problematic to deal with even if their presence is not required, such as Galvanized and Cohorts, and to cause Fixed Point shenanigans. &nbsp;But it's the first category that is most vital, because those are the ones that cannot be allowed to leave play for any reason. &nbsp;But I don't know of a way to make those cards leave play without destroying them. &nbsp;Villains such as Spite have ways of making hero cards leave play, but there are no ways other than Into the Stratosphere (which again can't target indestructable cards) of making Villain cards leave play, and the vital cards are so far exclusively Villain cards.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Therefore, allowing indestructable cards to leave play through methods other than destruction would not break the game, it would only reduce the schenanigans caused by Fixed Point. &nbsp;And while I like the craziness caused by Fixed Point, those interactions should be weighed against the fact that the ruling that creates them is unintuitive.</p>",
        "post_number": 27,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:45:17.140Z",
