      {
        "id": 164141,
        "name": "Arcanist Lupus",
        "username": "Arcanist_Lupus",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arcanist_lupus/{size}/2169_2.png",
        "created_at": "2016-03-04T17:32:19.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Matchstickman\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/matchstickman/48/1880_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Matchstickman:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<div><b>Arcanist Lupus wrote:</b><br><blockquote>I don't think that it's counterintutive so much as just unintuitive. &nbsp;People look at \"indestructible\" and assume that it means \"cannot be destroyed\", and there's absolutely no reason to believe that it would do anything else unless you look it up in the rulebook,\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote></div>\nThere are confusions with people asking if they can discard cards from play.Whatever the keyword used if you don't read the rules of a new game and apply logic from other games to it you are going to have problems.\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>That is certainly true. But:</p>\n<p>a) Asking if something works a certain way is different from assuming that it works a certain way. &nbsp;If you ask, you can check. &nbsp;If you assume, then you won't.</p>\n<p>b) Even if you've never played another game before you are likely to assume that \"Indestructible\" means \"cannot be destroyed\", because that is literally what \"Indestructible\" means. &nbsp;Not as a game term, but as a word. &nbsp;If you're using a word to mean something other than the literal definition of the word in a context where the literal defintion has valid meaning, then you've probably chosen the wrong word to use.</p>",
        "post_number": 36,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:45:17.980Z",
